    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Increase $\\$100$ by $10\\%$ and then decrease the result by $10\\%$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\$99$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Final amount}&=\\$100\\times1.1\\times0.9\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$110\\times0.9\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$99\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Increase $\\$100$ by $20\\%$ and then decrease the result by $20\\%$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\$96$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Final amount}&=\\$100\\times1.2\\times0.8\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$120\\times0.8\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$96\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Increase $\\$100$ by $30\\%$ and then decrease the result by $30\\%$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\$91$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Final amount}&=\\$100\\times1.3\\times0.7\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$130\\times0.7\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=\\$91\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "In each of the above calculations you will have found that the resulting amount was always less than the initial amount. Prove that this always happens. Hint: Set up the problem by assuming a $P\\%$ increase followed by a $P\\%$ decrease.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p10-q6-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The overall decrease is ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\frac{P^2}{100}\\%",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p10-q6-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Multiplying the two change multipliers gives ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1-\\frac{P^2}{10000}<1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " for a positive percentage change.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $x>0$ be the original amount and $P>0$ the percentage change.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Final amount}&=x\\left(1+\\frac{P}{100}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{P}{100}\\right)\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=x\\left(1-\\frac{P^2}{10000}\\right)\\\\\\text{Final amount}&=x-\\frac{xP^2}{10000}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $\\frac{xP^2}{10000}>0$, the final amount is less than $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
  },
